1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,842
CHAPTER 4 — THE WAR OF THE CURRENTS

2
00:00:04,192 --> 00:00:09,654
Word of his motor reached George Westinghouse, an industrialist who was betting on alternating current.

3
00:00:09,854 --> 00:00:17,931
He paid to use his patents and promised him two dollars and fifty cents for every horsepower sold. Remember that number.

4
00:00:18,381 --> 00:00:21,304
Why such a fierce battle? Back to the chalkboard.

5
00:00:21,504 --> 00:00:34,812
Wires lose energy as heat. The more current flows through them, the more they lose. But the same energy can travel as lots of current at low voltage… or very little current at high voltage.

6
00:00:35,012 --> 00:00:42,319
With a transformer, alternating current steps up the voltage to travel far. And steps it down before entering your home.

7
00:00:42,519 --> 00:00:49,289
Direct current back then couldn't do that. It needed a power station every mile or so.

8
00:00:49,489 --> 00:00:56,719
Edison's camp responded with fear: they electrocuted animals in public to show that alternating current killed.

9
00:00:56,919 --> 00:01:10,458
Tesla answered with a spectacle: he let ultra-high-frequency currents pass through his body and lit lamps with his bare hands. That only worked at extreme frequencies, and even then it was dangerous.

10
00:01:11,358 --> 00:01:16,896
Edison said alternating current killed. Tesla used it to light up his hands.
